-- title:
--   Subadditivity of the maximum norm
-- statement:
--   For $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ write $\|x\|_\infty = \sup_{i} |x_i|$ for the maximum (supremum) norm over the coordinates. Then for all $x,y \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
--   $$\|x+y\|_\infty \;\le\; \|x\|_\infty + \|y\|_\infty .$$
--
--   Together with homogeneity and non-negativity this is the triangle inequality that makes $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ a norm on $\mathbb{R}^d$; it is the workhorse behind every containment and separation estimate for cubes, which are defined as sublevel sets of $\|\cdot\|_\infty$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The underlying type is `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`, i.e. $\mathbb{R}^d$ with its Euclidean ($\ell^2$) norm; $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ is defined separately as the indexed supremum $\bigsqcup_i |x_i|$ of the coordinates, which for $d = 0$ is the supremum of the empty family, namely $0$.
-- source:
